ine $L2$, and by the definition of {{field-5}}.\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line {{field-6}} and $L5$ by the definition of {{field-7}}."}] :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Proofs of Inclusion Statements" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049468 :topic/math-academy-id 254 :topic/title "Proving Distributive Properties of the Cartesian Product"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059786 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the intersection of sets" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057489} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the union of sets" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057490} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the Cartesian product" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057491} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the difference of sets" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057492} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the set complement" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057493}] :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057488} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the union of sets" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057495} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the set complement" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057496} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the Cartesian product" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057497} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the difference of sets" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057498} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the intersection of sets" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057499}] :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the distributive law" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057501} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the commutative law" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057502} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the associative law" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057503}] :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057500}] :question/math-academy-id "q-185289" :question/problem "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Given that $A$, $B$, and $C$ are sets, $A \\cdot (B∪C) = (A \\cdot B)∪(A \\cdot C)$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $(x, y) ∈ A \\cdot (B∪C)$, which is equivalent to the following:\n> $L2$: $(x ∈ A)∧(y ∈ B∪C)$\n> $L3$: $(x ∈ A)∧(y ∈ B∨y ∈ C)$\n> $L4$: $(x ∈ A∧y ∈ B)∨(x ∈ A∧y ∈ C)$\n> $L5$: $(x, y) ∈ A \\cdot B∨(x, y) ∈ A \\cdot C$\n> $L6$: $(x, y) ∈ (A \\cdot B)∪(A \\cdot C)$\n> $L7$: Thus, $A \\cdot (B∪C) = (A \\cdot B)∪(A \\cdot C)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the definition of {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from line $L2$ by the definition of {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line $L3$ by {{field-3}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x ∈ (A∩B))∧(y ∈ C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057809} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x ∈ (A∪B))∧(y ∈ C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057810} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x ∈ (A∪B))∨(y ∈ C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057811} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x ∈ (A∩B))∨(y ∈ C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057812}] :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057808}] :question/math-academy-id "q-222137" :question/problem "Let $A$, $B$, and $C$ be sets. Prove that\n$(A∪B) \\cdot C = (A \\cdot C)∪(B \\cdot C)$.\n\nSupp